 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive densification originated with the introduction of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by Kerbl et al. in 2023, which established the baseline heuristic of splitting Gaussians with high gradients and large scales, and pruning those with low opacity [S1]. Subsequent works have refined this approach to address specific limitations: Mini-Splatting introduced blur splitting to improve perceptual metrics [S1]; HSPT introduced parallel respawning with edge-case handling for hierarchical structures [S2, unofficial]; I3DGS explored removing split operations to speed up training [S3, unofficial]; Mixed-Primitive methods extended densification to triangles and lines [S4, unofficial]; SLAM-based methods like those in [S5] introduced rendering-guided densification for real-time consistency; and Global-to-local frameworks [S6, unofficial] separated spatial distribution from local refinement to reduce computational cost.
+The concept of adaptive densification was introduced with the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1][S6, unofficial]. The original method established the heuristic-based split, clone, and prune cycle driven by gradient magnitude and opacity. Subsequent works have refined this process: Mini-Splatting introduced blur splitting to improve LPIPS [S1]; HSPT introduced parallel respawning with edge case handling for large-scale reconstruction [S2, unofficial]; I3DGS explored removing split operations to speed up training [S3, unofficial]; Mixed-Primitive methods extended densification to triangles and lines [S4, unofficial]; SLAM-based methods like the one in [S5] introduced rendering-guided densification for RGB-D consistency; and Global-to-Local frameworks [S6, unofficial] separated global spread from local refinement to reduce computational redundancy.
 
